module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.enforcement_gate_positioned_after_the_effect_it_guards

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data enforcement_gate_positioned_after_the_effect_it_guards: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "enforcement_gate_positioned_after_the_effect_it_guards" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**a refusal computed before the effect but ENFORCED after it is a classification wearing the costume of a wall, and the receipt then reports the refusal about a world the run already changed** (INVALID STATE: a check produces a correct verdict, the verdict is carried into a receipt, and the irreversible effect happens ANYWAY because nothing branches on the verdict before the write. RECOGNITION RULE: for each guard, find the effect it names and ask whether any path reaches that effect with the guard's refusing value in hand -- the tell is a computed refusal that is only ever READ at serialization time. WHY THE RECEIPT MAKES IT WORSE: the run exits non-zero carrying an accurate diagnostic, so the artifact looks like the wall working, and the load-bearing observation is EXTERNAL -- that nothing moved -- which no reader of the receipt performs. TWO INSTANCES IN ONE LANE, gunbc#10739. First: host_reset_return_attempt computed the observer self-identity, carried it into the receipt, and issued the reset regardless, so a job running on its own subject would have power-cycled the machine it was standing on and THEN reported the refusal. Second, found by review after the first was fixed and sharper for it: the repaired gate consulted the IDENTITY ONLY, so a refused PAIRING -- carrying ObserverIsSubject, SubjectNotRostered, ObserverNotRostered, NoPeerAvailable -- still fell through to the controller write, and the receipt then rendered `pairing-not-minted` about a run that had already written to a controller. THE SECOND IS THE INSTRUCTIVE ONE: the arm was unreachable in practice only because a CALLER pre-filtered, so the module presented as its wall an arm whose safety came from someone else, and the next caller would not have inherited it. RUNG FOUND AT: mitigatable. CEILING: structurally guaranteed -- the effect can be made to REQUIRE the guard's permitting value as an argument, so an unpermitted call has no form; the interim shape actually taken was one classifier returning the refusal cause itself, consumed by both the gate and the verdict, which removes the second authority but not the ordering hazard. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, a capability and not an artifact: an effect-ordering reader that, for a declared irreversible operation, refuses any path reaching it that does not first branch on the guard naming it -- sufficient to run over every `func` carrying an effect grant rather than one reviewed function. WHAT IS NOW HARDER TO DO THAN BEFORE: NOTHING. A record, not a repair; the class stands UNREMEDIED at mitigatable and may not be cited as coverage ([[a_written_row_is_not_a_firing_mechanism]]). WHY REVIEW CAUGHT IT TWICE AND THE AUTHOR DID NOT: the guard READS correct at its own site -- the defect is a relation between two statements several lines apart, and re-reading the guard confirms it every time. DISTINCT FROM [[declared_refusal_unreachable_from_its_own_constructor]]: there the arm is never reached; here it is reached, and reached too late.)",
    "SPECIMEN IN AN OPERATOR-AUTHORIZED ACTUATION, WITH THE EXPOSURE IT CAUSED (2026-09-13, Mt. Collins unit 1 BMC, receipt artifacts/bmc/mtcollins1-bmc-user3-route-2026-09-13.txt on gunbc#11189). A four-step script configured a dormant account: set its password, enable it, grant channel access, grant administrator privilege. The first step returned the correct refusing verdict -- 'IPMI command failed: Unspecified error / Set User Password command failed' -- and nothing branched on it, so the three effects ran and the account became an enabled ADMINISTRATOR holding its PRIOR password, one the operator did not control, from 00:17:00Z until rollback at 00:17:10Z. The verdict was computed before the effect and printed; it was enforced by nobody. The refusal was also CORRECT ABOUT A CAUSE the script could not see: a password-complexity policy, not an immutable account, which a later discriminator on the disabled account established. So the unconsumed verdict was not noise to be retried past; it was the one fact that mattered. THE REPAIR WAS POSITIONAL, and cheap: the corrected script aborts on a set that does not report success, before any enable runs. A POST-ROLLBACK LOGIN TEST IS NOT EVIDENCE ABOUT THE WINDOW, and is recorded so it is not reused as such: with channel access removed every password is rejected, so 'prior password rejected' could not discriminate whether the window was exploitable -- which is the sibling class [[absent_reads_identically_to_never_looked]], arriving in the cleanup of this one.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
